1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,513
The final test of this course has
a rule that answers that engineer.

2
00:00:08,553 --> 00:00:13,520
In every debugging and incident item, a
state-changing command given before the root

3
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:17,752
cause is established loses half of
the item's points, whatever follows.

4
00:00:17,792 --> 00:00:22,640
A state-changing command alters the repository
instead of only reading it, and the

5
00:00:22,680 --> 00:00:27,349
root cause is the mechanism behind the
symptom. A brilliant fix, reached by the

6
00:00:27,389 --> 00:00:30,806
right instinct, after the wrong
first move, is worth half.

7
00:00:31,146 --> 00:00:36,007
The test measures the standard the course
was built for: given a state or a symptom,

8
00:00:36,047 --> 00:00:40,734
you name the mechanism, show the evidence,
choose the lowest-risk fix, verify it,

9
00:00:40,774 --> 00:00:44,527
and say what prevents a repeat.
Recalling a command earns little.

10
00:00:44,567 --> 00:00:48,962
Keep that engineer's sentence in mind.
By the end you'll have a reply to it.

11
00:00:50,802 --> 00:00:54,564
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:54,604 --> 00:00:59,737
This is a briefing for the final knowledge
test, taken in Module 40 after the ninth

13
00:00:59,777 --> 00:01:03,289
mastery gate. It covers the
whole book, Chapters 1 to 30.

14
00:01:03,329 --> 00:01:08,400
I show you the rules and nothing else: the
page of the test file that says how the

15
00:01:08,440 --> 00:01:13,386
test is taken, up to and not including
Section 1. No item of the test appears in

16
00:01:13,426 --> 00:01:14,072
this video.

17
00:01:14,412 --> 00:01:18,558
Four things: the structure, the rules,
the sittings and the pass rule.

18
00:01:18,598 --> 00:01:23,581
Then how to prepare between sittings, and
when the answer key comes into your hands.

19
00:01:25,421 --> 00:01:26,999
Here's what you'll walk away with.

20
00:01:27,339 --> 00:01:28,751
After this video you can:

21
00:01:29,091 --> 00:01:34,109
State the structure of the final test:
eighteen sections, eight item types, six

22
00:01:34,149 --> 00:01:34,686
sittings.

23
00:01:35,026 --> 00:01:36,272
State the pass rule.

24
00:01:36,612 --> 00:01:41,285
Explain the rule that a state-changing
command before the root cause costs half an

25
00:01:41,325 --> 00:01:41,572
item.

26
00:01:41,912 --> 00:01:44,697
Plan the six sittings and
the revision before each.

27
00:01:45,037 --> 00:01:48,327
Use the answer key only as
the retake procedure allows.

28
00:01:50,167 --> 00:01:53,949
The structure. The test has 250
items in eighteen sections.

29
00:01:53,989 --> 00:01:59,261
The sections are the eighteen areas of the
question bank, from Fundamentals to the

30
00:01:59,301 --> 00:02:00,168
CTO interview.

31
00:02:00,508 --> 00:02:01,987
There are eight types of item.

32
00:02:02,327 --> 00:02:06,065
For each, the table gives the count,
the points and how it is answered.

33
00:02:08,405 --> 00:02:14,125
Quick quiz, from the table. Which type
carries the most points in total: A, multiple

34
00:02:14,165 --> 00:02:18,240
choice, B, debugging, or C, the
practical labs? Your answer?

35
00:02:23,080 --> 00:02:29,727
B. Look at where the points are. Multiple
choice is 86 items and 86 points of 804.

36
00:02:29,767 --> 00:02:36,577
The debugging items alone are 168 points,
the oral items 164, the incident responses

37
00:02:36,617 --> 00:02:43,183
96, the practical labs 90. The test is
weighted toward explaining, diagnosing and

38
00:02:43,223 --> 00:02:43,672
doing.

39
00:02:44,012 --> 00:02:46,515
The rules. Six, from the test file.

40
00:02:46,855 --> 00:02:51,759
One. Closed book, except in the practical
labs, where git help <command> and the

41
00:02:51,799 --> 00:02:54,787
manual pages are allowed
and the textbook is not.

42
00:02:55,127 --> 00:02:59,020
Two. Where an item says "no terminal",
a terminal is a failed item.

43
00:02:59,060 --> 00:03:02,013
The prediction items are
worthless if you run them.

44
00:03:02,053 --> 00:03:05,770
They measure whether the model in
your head produces the output.

45
00:03:05,810 --> 00:03:08,001
A terminal measures nothing about you.

46
00:03:08,341 --> 00:03:12,751
Three. In prediction items you write <id>
where an abbreviated commit ID would

47
00:03:12,791 --> 00:03:17,543
appear. Lines that start with a hash sign
in a transcript are comments that describe

48
00:03:17,583 --> 00:03:19,483
hidden setup or what a person did.

49
00:03:19,823 --> 00:03:24,654
Four. In debugging and incident items, a
state-changing command given before the

50
00:03:24,694 --> 00:03:29,283
root cause is established loses half of
the item's points, whatever follows.

51
00:03:29,323 --> 00:03:32,571
The read-only phase is the
first thing that is marked.

52
00:03:32,911 --> 00:03:37,807
Five. GitHub-side items are answered from
what you know of the documented behavior.

53
00:03:37,847 --> 00:03:41,970
Nothing in the test requires a GitHub
account or a network connection.

54
00:03:42,310 --> 00:03:47,230
Six. The practical labs follow the generator
protocol of the incident drills: run

55
00:03:47,270 --> 00:03:52,191
the generator, open the lab shell at the
path it prints, work, then run the check

56
00:03:52,231 --> 00:03:57,091
from the course root. Running the generator
again rebuilds the lab from nothing.

57
00:03:57,131 --> 00:04:02,113
A lab counts when its check prints PASS
and the hand-in listed on its task card is

58
00:04:02,153 --> 00:04:05,542
complete: 6 points for the
end state, 4 for the hand-in.

59
00:04:05,882 --> 00:04:11,234
Why rule four exists. It's the first fact
of Chapter 29 made into a marking scheme:

60
00:04:11,274 --> 00:04:15,651
most damage is done after the
incident, by the first repair attempt.

61
00:04:15,691 --> 00:04:19,419
The rule doesn't ask you to be
slow. It asks for an order.

62
00:04:19,759 --> 00:04:24,586
A fixed opening is what replaces confidence,
and the study the textbook cites found

63
00:04:24,626 --> 00:04:29,396
that experienced users were strongly
represented among those asking Git questions.

64
00:04:29,436 --> 00:04:34,146
The ritual isn't for juniors. It's for
anyone whose repository contains something

65
00:04:34,186 --> 00:04:38,604
that exists nowhere else. That's the
reply to the engineer from the opening.

66
00:04:38,944 --> 00:04:41,881
The sittings. Six, in this
order, on separate days.

67
00:04:42,221 --> 00:04:48,001
The pass rule. Three conditions, all
required. 85 percent of the total: 684 of 804

68
00:04:48,041 --> 00:04:53,394
points. At least 70 percent in every
section. And at least seven of the nine

69
00:04:53,434 --> 00:04:55,949
practical labs with a passing check.

70
00:04:56,289 --> 00:05:01,266
After a section below 70 percent. That
section is retaken alone, after the restudy

71
00:05:01,306 --> 00:05:06,343
that the answer key names for each item.
The labs are retaken by regenerating them.

72
00:05:06,683 --> 00:05:11,485
The answer key. The test file contains no
answers. You receive the key after you've

73
00:05:11,525 --> 00:05:16,094
handed in the whole test. Before that it
isn't opened, and between sittings you

74
00:05:16,134 --> 00:05:17,786
don't read ahead in the test.

75
00:05:19,626 --> 00:05:24,626
A picture helps. Think of the test as the
course in miniature, folded so that every

76
00:05:24,666 --> 00:05:29,606
part shows at once. Each section asks the
same five things about a different area:

77
00:05:29,646 --> 00:05:33,492
the state, the mechanism, the
evidence, the fix, the prevention.

78
00:05:33,532 --> 00:05:38,533
The eight item types are eight ways of
asking them: choose it, predict it, draw it,

79
00:05:38,573 --> 00:05:41,265
read it, diagnose it,
do it, lead it, say it.

80
00:05:41,605 --> 00:05:45,306
Where this breaks: a miniature
suggests that a quick look is enough.

81
00:05:45,346 --> 00:05:49,871
The time budget says otherwise: about
twenty-one and a half hours, in six sittings.

82
00:05:49,911 --> 00:05:54,491
It's the longest assessment in the course,
and it's built so that it can't be passed

83
00:05:54,531 --> 00:05:56,141
by a good memory for commands.

84
00:05:56,481 --> 00:05:59,902
A second picture, for rule
four: a surgeon's checklist.

85
00:05:59,942 --> 00:06:04,873
Senior surgeons use it. Its value doesn't
come from the surgeon not knowing the

86
00:06:04,913 --> 00:06:09,278
steps. It comes from the cases where
the surgeon is certain and wrong.

87
00:06:11,118 --> 00:06:14,474
Six sittings, with their
sections and time budgets.

88
00:06:14,514 --> 00:06:19,669
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds.
Write the numbers one to six, and a date

89
00:06:19,709 --> 00:06:24,065
beside each, on separate days.
Which sitting gets your freest day?

90
00:06:24,105 --> 00:06:25,130
Say it out loud.

91
00:06:29,970 --> 00:06:34,425
Sitting 5: the heaviest, with the most
points. Plan it for a day on which you have

92
00:06:34,465 --> 00:06:38,481
nothing else. Then add the revision
you'll do the day before each sitting.

93
00:06:40,321 --> 00:06:45,272
On screen is the opening of the test file,
and its section "How the test is taken".

94
00:06:45,312 --> 00:06:50,082
I stop at the line under the pass rule.
Section 1 begins below that line, and it

95
00:06:50,122 --> 00:06:50,804
isn't shown.

96
00:06:51,144 --> 00:06:54,338
Into the lab, for one snippet
that isn't from the test.

97
00:06:54,378 --> 00:06:59,161
The form of a practical lab has three parts:
a generated repository, a task, and a

98
00:06:59,201 --> 00:07:02,984
check script that verifies the end
state with read-only commands.

99
00:07:03,024 --> 00:07:07,924
In this snippet somebody chose a recovery
that looked right, and then ran the check.

100
00:07:08,264 --> 00:07:12,917
🔴 DANGEROUS: git reset --hard. It moves
the branch, replaces the index and

101
00:07:12,957 --> 00:07:16,912
overwrites the working tree, and
uncommitted work is destroyed.

102
00:07:16,952 --> 00:07:21,923
Preview with git status and git diff. In
the lab it's the tempting move, run on

103
00:07:21,963 --> 00:07:22,430
purpose.

104
00:07:22,770 --> 00:07:28,028
Predict before the transcript appears: the
reset brings the three commits back, and

105
00:07:28,068 --> 00:07:31,412
the check still says "NOT
YET". Why? Say it out loud.

106
00:07:39,252 --> 00:07:42,982
The three commits are back,
and the result is "NOT YET".

107
00:07:43,022 --> 00:07:48,436
You know the answer from video 186. The
branch was moved back, so the commit made

108
00:07:48,476 --> 00:07:52,476
after the accident left it. The
staged file wasn't restored.

109
00:07:52,516 --> 00:07:56,044
And the relation to main is
wrong. Four failed lines.

110
00:07:56,384 --> 00:08:00,728
That's what a practical lab in the
final test looks like from the outside.

111
00:08:00,768 --> 00:08:04,875
The check prints one line per condition
and ends with PASS or NOT YET.

112
00:08:04,915 --> 00:08:09,555
And the lesson of this snippet is the
lesson of rule six: a lab counts when the

113
00:08:09,595 --> 00:08:14,532
check passes and the hand-in is complete.
Six points for the end state, four for the

114
00:08:14,572 --> 00:08:18,264
hand-in. A passing end state
without the hand-in is six of ten.

115
00:08:20,104 --> 00:08:21,752
Five mistakes to watch for.

116
00:08:22,092 --> 00:08:26,792
Running a prediction item "to be sure".
Root cause: the item measures the model in

117
00:08:26,832 --> 00:08:31,648
your head, and with a terminal it measures
nothing, so the rule counts it as failed.

118
00:08:31,988 --> 00:08:36,663
Writing the fix first in a debugging item.
Root cause: the read-only phase is the

119
00:08:36,703 --> 00:08:41,553
first thing that is marked, and a state-changing
command before the root cause costs

120
00:08:41,593 --> 00:08:42,368
half the item.

121
00:08:42,708 --> 00:08:48,203
Aiming at the total. Root cause: the pass
rule also requires 70 percent in every

122
00:08:48,243 --> 00:08:52,976
section and seven of nine labs, so
one neglected area fails the test.

123
00:08:53,316 --> 00:08:57,910
Reading ahead between sittings. Root cause:
an item that has been read cannot be

124
00:08:57,950 --> 00:09:01,964
answered as an unseen item, and the
sittings are designed around that.

125
00:09:02,304 --> 00:09:07,072
Treating a passing check as a finished lab.
Root cause: four of a lab's ten points

126
00:09:07,112 --> 00:09:09,241
are for the hand-in on its task card.

127
00:09:11,081 --> 00:09:15,566
Now, out of the lab. An engineer plans
the final test the way she would plan a

128
00:09:15,606 --> 00:09:19,047
release. Six sittings on six
separate days over three weeks.

129
00:09:19,087 --> 00:09:23,921
The day before each sitting, a revision of
the sections it covers, with the revision

130
00:09:23,961 --> 00:09:28,794
checklist and the cheat sheet, closed
afterwards. The longest sitting on a Saturday.

131
00:09:29,134 --> 00:09:34,293
After sitting 2 she knows, without any key,
that her rebase predictions were slow:

132
00:09:34,333 --> 00:09:38,351
she had to reconstruct what --onto
takes as arguments each time.

133
00:09:38,391 --> 00:09:43,487
She doesn't read ahead. She restudies
exactly that, with the chapter and its lab,

134
00:09:43,527 --> 00:09:44,565
before sitting 3.

135
00:09:44,905 --> 00:09:49,969
When the whole test is handed in and scored,
one section is at 66 percent: remote

136
00:09:50,009 --> 00:09:55,200
workflows. The answer key now reaches her,
with the restudy named for each item she

137
00:09:55,240 --> 00:10:00,430
lost. She does that restudy, retakes the
one section alone, and regenerates the one

138
00:10:00,470 --> 00:10:02,384
lab whose check had not passed.

139
00:10:02,724 --> 00:10:07,398
Nothing else is repeated. Her comment
afterwards is about rule four: twice she had

140
00:10:07,438 --> 00:10:11,997
caught herself writing a reset as the
first line of a debugging answer, and both

141
00:10:12,037 --> 00:10:16,826
times the read-only commands she wrote
instead showed that the reset would have been

142
00:10:16,866 --> 00:10:17,631
the wrong fix.

143
00:10:19,471 --> 00:10:23,606
Your turn. Revise with
revision/revision-checklist.md and

144
00:10:23,646 --> 00:10:29,465
cheatsheets/git-cheat-sheet.md, for the
sections of sitting 1: Fundamentals, Git

145
00:10:29,505 --> 00:10:33,860
internals, Branching. Then put
both away and take sitting 1.

146
00:10:34,200 --> 00:10:38,861
Before you start, predict your score for
each of its three sections and write the

147
00:10:38,901 --> 00:10:42,692
three numbers down. After the whole
test has been scored, compare.

148
00:10:42,732 --> 00:10:47,045
During the sitting, obey the item's
conditions literally: where it says "no

149
00:10:47,085 --> 00:10:48,787
terminal", close the terminal.

150
00:10:49,127 --> 00:10:54,363
The challenge is the whole test:
assessments/final-test.md, six sittings, in order,

151
00:10:54,403 --> 00:10:55,444
on separate days.

152
00:10:57,284 --> 00:10:59,985
Read the question, then answer
out loud, in two minutes.

153
00:11:04,825 --> 00:11:09,326
A strong answer doesn't argue from
authority and doesn't insult the colleague.

154
00:11:09,366 --> 00:11:14,158
It gives evidence: what the textbook reports
about who asks Git questions, and when

155
00:11:14,198 --> 00:11:18,583
in an incident the damage is usually
done. It says what the ritual costs, in

156
00:11:18,623 --> 00:11:23,182
seconds, and what it protects, in terms
of state that exists in one place only.

157
00:11:23,522 --> 00:11:27,770
It concedes what is true in the objection:
there are cases where the full method

158
00:11:27,810 --> 00:11:32,217
isn't needed, and you can name them. And
it ends on a control, not on a moral: what

159
00:11:32,257 --> 00:11:35,647
you would do in a team where one
person won't follow the ritual.

160
00:11:37,487 --> 00:11:38,414
Let's land this.

161
00:11:38,754 --> 00:11:44,893
The final test has 250 items in eighteen
sections and eight item types, worth 804

162
00:11:44,933 --> 00:11:48,479
points, taken in six
sittings on separate days.

163
00:11:48,819 --> 00:11:54,498
You pass with 85 percent overall, at least
70 percent in every section, and at least

164
00:11:54,538 --> 00:11:57,017
seven of nine practical labs passing.

165
00:11:57,357 --> 00:12:01,262
"No terminal" is literal, and a prediction
item that was run is worthless.

166
00:12:01,602 --> 00:12:06,544
In debugging and incident items a state-changing
command before the root cause loses

167
00:12:06,584 --> 00:12:07,375
half the item.

168
00:12:07,715 --> 00:12:12,067
The answer key arrives after the whole
test is handed in; a weak section is

169
00:12:12,107 --> 00:12:14,702
restudied as the key
names and retaken alone.

170
00:12:16,542 --> 00:12:21,354
Between sittings, restudy only what the
previous sitting showed to be weak, and

171
00:12:21,394 --> 00:12:26,513
don't read ahead in the test. Keep a one-line
note after each sitting: the area that

172
00:12:26,553 --> 00:12:31,550
cost you the most time, and the chapter
section you'll reread before the next one.

173
00:12:31,890 --> 00:12:36,717
You now know the whole shape of the final
test: what it asks, how it's marked, and

174
00:12:36,757 --> 00:12:40,754
the one order it insists on. Put
your six dates in a calendar today.

175
00:12:40,794 --> 00:12:44,553
Next time: the capstone, eight
incidents at a fictional company.

176
00:12:44,593 --> 00:12:47,640
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

177
00:12:47,680 --> 00:12:49,065
See you in the next one.
